  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1257 PM MDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nWET WEATHER WITH FLOODING POTENTIAL THROUGH TONIGHT:\n\nSatellite imagery currently shows the low pressure system \nresponsible for our recent bout of wet weather centered in \nnorthwest New Mexico. The low will continue to eject \neast/northeast as the day wears on, leading to one final round \nof precipitation through this evening. With an overcast stratus\ncloud deck in place, organized convection will not be favored \nfor most locations. Instead, steady light to moderate rain with \npockets of locally heavy rainfall will persist. \n\nGiven much of Southwest Colorado has already received well over\nan inch of rainfall over the last 48 hours, additional rain \nwill have the potential to produce localized flooding. As a \nresult, the Flood Watch will continue for the San Juan Mountains\nand San Juan River Basin through 6 AM Thursday morning. \n\nMost shower and storm activity will decrease in coverage this \nevening as the low moves east and drier air moves in behind it \nfrom the northwest. \n\nWARM AND DRY THURSDAY THROUGH THE WEEKEND:\n\nHigh pressure moves into the region on Thursday. This pattern \nshift will bring dry and warmer weather through at least the \nweekend. High temperatures are forecast to climb from near \nnormal on Thursday to 5 to 10 degrees above normal by the \nweekend.",
